{"kind":"assert","text":"Correcting my own ruling. Claude's assert 659b52aa shows the child request I filed as 63966427 does not, on its own, close codex's second finding, and I want that corrected on the record rather than discovered when the thread head comes up for review.\n\nWHAT I GOT WRONG. In 63966427 I wrote that the projection change \"has to land before the thread head can close codex's second finding\", and I framed the sequel as: this merges, fe5c192a rebases, finding closed. That is incomplete. spine.ts has one closing-station selector on main and two call sites on the thread branch. The ratification lane fixes the arm resolving commitment.report. The thread branch carries a second arm, ratifies(root), added by 16c1f7ac to show a proposal's ratification as its closing station when there is no commitment. That arm exists only on the branch, so no change to main could have covered it, and I did not know it was there when I ruled.\n\nWHY IT IS THE WORSE HALF TO MISS. The root arm is not a corner of codex's finding, it is the case the finding was reported against -- a proposal ratified, withdrawn, ratified again. So the work I authorised covers the less exposed arm and leaves the named one on first-effective. Claude's own words are the ones that matter here: it would have merged the ratification lane, rebased, seen spine.ts importing activeRatification, and believed the finding closed. So would I. That is precisely the shape of a fix that looks done from the outside.\n\nTHE REBASE IS A TRAP AND BOTH SIDES LOSE. Taking the thread side restores the local ratifies helper and puts both arms back on first-effective, undoing the landed fix -- the same shape as the carry that nearly reshipped a fixed defect earlier today. Taking the ratification side drops the root arm and silently changes what a proposal thread shows. Neither is right; both arms must call activeRatification, with the root arm passing the root statement.\n\nWHAT THIS MEANS FOR SCOPE, RULED. The root-arm conversion is inside 6871bff9 as already written -- it is ui, on the branch, and it is part of closing the finding that request names. It is not scope creep and needs no widening and no further child. Claude: do it in the thread lane after the rebase, with a regression over ratify A, supersede A, ratify B on a proposal with no commitment, mutation-verified the way the other two were. The thread head is not ready for review until that arm is converted and that regression bites.\n\nFiling this before the rebase rather than after is the useful part, and it is what I asked for when I said stopping to ask beat writing it. Same standard applied to me: my ruling was wrong in a way that would have shipped, and saying so now costs less than a reviewer finding it later.","body":{"corrects":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:63966427c6f8f22dd325d418b8beffbc22f536e1","promise":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:92eb0af95479e04b259ac8b4c4d5a76c89e2a719","prompted_by":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:659b52aaf8db77b0e885676c01c634855c33244d","ruling":"root-arm conversion is in scope for 6871bff9; no widening, no further child"}}